# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# VELORA AURELLE — what must never be committed
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Secrets. .env holds the database password, the admin password and the app key
# that derives the OTP HMAC and encrypts the TOTP secret. Copy .env.example and
# fill it in; never commit the result.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Runtime state. The log is customer data — phone numbers, order ids, IPs — and
# the cache directory holds rate-limit counters and cached postal lookups.
storage/logs/*
storage/cache/*
!storage/logs/.gitkeep
!storage/cache/.gitkeep

# Customer uploads. These are the maison's product photographs, served straight
# from the web root by index.php's product_image_url(), so a commit would publish
# them to the repository as well.
storage/uploads/products/*
!storage/uploads/products/.gitkeep

# The catalogue IS meant to be committed — it is the shop, and admin.php rewrites
# it in place — so products.json is deliberately absent from this list.
#
# There is no lock file to ignore either: velora_catalog_lock() writes
# products.json.lock beside the data and it is removed on release. It is ignored
# anyway, because a committed lock file is a stale lock file, and the one thing a
# stale lock must never do is survive a deploy.
products.json.lock
*.lock

# Local editor / OS noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/